## 3–5 Reusable Findings

1. **Three concrete policy recommendations** (feeds S6): The paper recommends (i) the FSB conduct a formal review of 3LoD implementation across major banking/insurance groups to identify good vs. poor practice, (ii) the Basel Committee and IAIS issue guidance on designing 3LoD frameworks to clarify inter-line relationships, define borderlines, and ensure risk coverage, and (iii) board risk committees formally review 3LoD operation within their organisations — a three-tier (global standard-setter / sectoral regulator / board) recommendation template directly reusable for AI governance reform proposals.

2. **Governance-regulation mutual reinforcement framing** (feeds S6): Citing the Basel Committee's Corporate Governance Principles for Banks (2015), the paper frames sound corporate governance and prudential regulation as "ideally mutually reinforcing," with the board risk committee as the key linking mechanism — supports arguments that AI governance frameworks (e.g., RBI FREE-AI) should be assessed for board-risk-committee integration, not just standalone policy issuance.

3. **US sub-threshold risk committee gap (⚠ VERIFY)** (feeds S6): The paper notes US banks below $10 billion in assets are not required to maintain a separate risk committee (citing FIS 2016), and that the US does not follow Basel Committee recommendations on this point — a useful comparative data point on regulatory non-convergence for [[Regulatory_Parallelism]], but the $10bn threshold and FIS 2016 citation should be ⚠ VERIFIED before external use given the source's age (2018, citing 2016 data).

